#: src/read-documentation/search.md:90
msgid "Non-functions in type-based search"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:91
msgid ""
"Certain items that are not functions are treated as though they were a "
"semantically equivalent function."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:94
msgid ""
"For example, struct fields are treated as though they were getter methods. "
"This means that a search for `CpuidResult -> u32` will show the "
"`CpuidResult::eax` field in the results."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:98
msgid ""
"Additionally, `const` and `static` items are treated as nullary functions, "
"so `-> u32` will match `u32::MAX`."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:101
msgid "How type-based search works"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:103
msgid ""
"In a complex type-based search, Rustdoc always treats every item's name as "
"literal. If a name is used and nothing in the docs matches the individual "
"item, such as a typo-ed [`uize -> vec`](../../std/vec/struct.Vec.html?"
"search=uize%20-%3E%20vec&filter-crate=std) search, the item `uize` is "
"treated as a generic type parameter (resulting in `vec::from` and other "
"generic vec constructors)."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:110
msgid ""
"After deciding which items are type parameters and which are actual types, "
"it then searches by matching up the function parameters (written before the "
"`->`) and the return types (written after the `->`). Type matching is order-"
"agnostic, and allows items to be left out of the query, but items that are "
"present in the query must be present in the function for it to match. The "
"`self` parameter is treated the same as any other parameter, and `Self` is "
"resolved to the underlying type's name."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:118
msgid ""
"Function signature searches can query generics, wrapped in angle brackets, "
"and traits will be normalized like types in the search engine if no type "
"parameters match them. For example, a function with the signature `fn "
"my_function<I: Iterator<Item=u32>>(input: I) -> usize` can be matched with "
"the following queries:"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:124
msgid "`Iterator<Item=u32> -> usize`"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:125
msgid "`Iterator<u32> -> usize` (you can leave out the `Item=` part)"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:126
msgid "`Iterator -> usize` (you can leave out iterator's generic entirely)"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:127
msgid "`T -> usize` (you can match with a generic parameter)"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:129
msgid ""
"Each of the above queries is progressively looser, except the last one would "
"not match `dyn Iterator`, since that's not a type parameter."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:132
msgid ""
"If a bound has multiple associated types, specifying the name allows you to "
"pick which one gets matched. If no name is specified, then the query will "
"match of any of them. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:141
msgid ""
"/// This function can be found using the following search queries:\n"
"///\n"
"///     MyTrait<First=u8, Second=u32> -> bool\n"
"///     MyTrait<Second=u32> -> bool\n"
"///\n"
"/// The following queries, however, will *not* match it:\n"
"///\n"
"///     MyTrait<First=u32> -> bool\n"
"///     MyTrait<u32, u32> -> bool\n"
"///     MyTrait<u32, First=u8> -> bool\n"
"///     MyTrait<u32, u8> -> bool\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:156
msgid ""
"Function parameters are order-agnostic, but sensitive to nesting and number "
"of matches. For example, a function with the signature `fn read_all(&mut "
"self: impl Read) -> Result<Vec<u8>, Error>` will match these queries:"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:161
msgid "`&mut Read -> Result<Vec<u8>, Error>`"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:162
msgid "`Read -> Result<Vec<u8>, Error>`"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:163
msgid "`Read -> Result<Vec<u8>>`"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:164
msgid "`Read -> Vec<u8>`"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:166
msgid ""
"But it _does not_ match `Result<Vec, u8>` or `Result<u8<Vec>>`, because "
"those are nested incorrectly, and it does not match `Result<Error, Vec<u8>>` "
"or `Result<Error>`, because those are in the wrong order. It also does not "
"match `Read -> u8`, because only [certain generic wrapper types](#wrappers-"
"that-can-be-omitted) can be left out, and `Vec` isn't one of them."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:175
msgid ""
"To search for a function that accepts a function as a parameter, like "
"`Iterator::all`, wrap the nested signature in parenthesis, as in "
"[`Iterator<T>, (T -> bool) -> bool`](../../std/vec/struct.Vec.html?"
"search=Iterator<T>%2C+(T+->+bool)+->+bool&filter-crate=std). You can also "
"search for a specific closure trait, such as `Iterator<T>, (FnMut(T) -> "
"bool) -> bool`, but you need to know which one you want."
msgstr ""

